time now for a final guess and there  were yet more allegations of misconduct  in Westminster this week from a  conservative MP losing the whip  following a complaint made to police the  claims of sleaze around the awarding of  PPE contracts during the pandemic it all  adds up to a constant drip drip of  stories ranging from bullying and  harassment to rule breaking and serious  sexual misconduct in recent years so is  there a particular problem in  Westminster politics and what is going  wrong well one person who might have a  view Sonya Khan who was a special  advisor to not one but two conservative  chancellors and Philip Hammond and Sajid  Java she settled an unfair dismissal  claim having been marched out of Downing  Street and has spoken of the environment  of bullying and bad behavior very good  to talk to you today Sonia  um are you surprised by what seems to be  a constant stream of these kind of  allegations and cases that are still  coming out  um I'm not surprised I think that over  the last few years we have created  um a system where actually if people  feel much more empowered to take action  I mean it's not perfect but I think  um what we have is a Westminster that  hasn't really changed but we have um a  group of people now  um that can work together or have seen  um examples of other people who've taken  action and feel comfortable to be able  to say actually this behavior is not  okay this isn't acceptable I feel like  there is much more of a standard now  about what good and bad behavior looks  like um in Westminster whereas I don't  think that quite existed before but we  have had a number of inquiries you know  Dame Laura Cox inquiry 2018  um 2020 to Alexander Allen's report and  to put into pretty Patel now there's  some inquiring to Dominic Robb do you  think that they actually change anything  do you think they're worth the paper  they're written on  um it's I mean it's difficult to say  because in my circumstance I had to um  take legal action in order to to change  behaviors or to get people to look at  what actually went wrong which isn't the  ideal solution because it's quite a  costly process  um I mean I think in truth we have a  system where the only change that  happens is when it comes to general  elections so I guess you could say  there's a way for constituents to change  who their MPS if they think that  actually the story is about bullying are  right but if you're a victim of bullying  whether you're in the civil service or  whether you're someone who works in  Westminster or quite a junior role you  work in Parliament then there isn't  really a way to change things other than  you changing so often that means people  have to leave their job or they leave  the sector or they go do something  completely different I've known people  who've become doctors or have gone to  work for Charities because I don't like  the culture of of Westminster so you I  guess there are elements that can be  changed but for most people the the way  that change happens is they have to  change the role themselves and we've  seen of course a number of politicians  male and female saying that they're not  going to stand again in in this current  election but quite a lot of them  relatively young female MPS do you think  political culture could have played a  part in that  I do and I think that the scrutiny that  many female MPS have is far greater than  male MPS who've seen people's romantic  lives uh being dissected uh in  newspapers we've seen some of their sort  of fashion choices dissected and I feel  like for a lot of them it must come to  the point where they decide that they  don't want that anymore  um so I can totally understand that and  I can also see why politics perhaps  doesn't always feel very rewarding in  this current environment and how maybe  they can affect change in perhaps  slightly different roles  there will be some though that will say  look you know politics like lots of jobs  is a high pressure environment there are  going to be big egos there are going to  be long hours people are going to be  exhausted strong personalities it's all  part of the job if you want to work in  that environment you just have to  toughen up a bit  um there is that sense and I would argue  you can have that in so many careers you  can have that in law you can have that  in medicine but the biggest difference  between those careers and politics is  that it isn't meritocratic so you can be  the smartest person you could have  dedicated your life to public service  for 40 or 50 years but that's not  necessarily what's going to help you  succeed it's a very very reputation  heavy and I think that's where the  frustration can come from from a lot of  people so it's much more about do you  know the right person are you in favor  with the right person or do your actions  sort of work for the current government  um and and I can see why that element of  the job perhaps isn't very attractive  and I think that's perhaps the  distinction to meet between other  careers that you can succeed just by  working hard whereas in politics there's  a bit more of a game to be played  so it seems that you're suggesting it's  riddled with cronyism  I mean I think that  the prize to anyone  as well it's very much based on kind of  who you know rather than than what you  know I mean you do need a base level of  um probably intelligence to be able to  get on especially with scrutiny that  comes through select committees and  whatever but yeah primarily it's oh have  you voted the right way do you know the  right people  so it's it's a you know rather than what  you know there is still no independent  advisor on ethics Liz truss said that  you know she knew what ethics were and  she didn't need one do you think that  that there should be somebody in place  in that role and soon  I don't think that an advisor on ethics  is going to fix the system that we have  I think it's much more structural than  that  um I think I've said before that there  are always people at the bottom in  politics and I don't think you can  change that but you do need to look at  how do you create a system that  encourages those people to speak up and  and does in a way that makes them feel  very protected so I'm talking about you  know lots of the Pas secretaries kind of  the people who bear the brunt of bad  behavior often  um and I feel like sometimes these roles  only focus or engage with the very very  seniors so how much having an  independent advisor will help perhaps  those who are going to struggle the most  I I don't know I feel like there's a  much more sort of wholesale change that  needs to happen but it needs to be  bottom up and and you yourself have  moved on you've got a new career uh  you've got a a new baby if you've known  how bad it would be and and looking back  would you have done it would you have  would you have still done gone the route  you've gone  um it's it's a good question I mean I do  have a baby and I'm not sure I would  want my sons to take the same path but  at the same time um I feel really proud  to have achieved change whether it's  change for women or period poverty or  whether it's trying to just represent a  different class of people  um to senior people like Prime Ministers  and chancellors so I guess that's a half  answer I'm grateful for what for what I  was able to do but perhaps right now I  wouldn't be encouraging a small child  into politics and this  um there is a major change about how  people are supported  case and you can't it's been lovely to  talk to you thanks a lot former special  advisors Sajid Javid and Philip Hammond  thank you
 

